course: dictionary
lesson: 54
title: Accusations and fairness
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An accusation is when someone says a person did something wrong or bad, such as stealing or lying. The word comes from the verb accuse, so an accusation is the claim itself. «Serious accusations belong to courts and investigations, not to jokes in the playground.»
explain: An accusation can be true or false, but either way it still has to be proved. In the nursery rhyme, «The Knave of Hearts, he stole those tarts» is an accusation of stealing. The rhyme only tells us what was said. Saying something does not make it true.
explain: Be fair when you hear one. «Never accuse someone because "everyone says"; accusations are for a teacher to look into.» Listen to both sides and look for evidence, the facts that show what really happened. Watch for the phrases «make an accusation», «a false accusation», «deny an accusation» and «face an accusation».
fact: Accusation is a noun; the verb is accuse.
fact: An accusation is a claim that someone did something wrong or bad.
fact: An accusation can be true or false, and it must still be proved.
fact: To deny an accusation means to say that it is not true.
fact: In citizenship lessons, people accused of crimes are «innocent until proven guilty».
exercise: «Serious accusations belong to courts and investigations, not to jokes in the playground.» What is an accusation? | A kind of joke told in the playground | *A claim that someone did something wrong | A type of court building | A happy song
exercise: A friend says, «You stole my pencil!» What is the word for what your friend has made? | A reward | A question about the weather | A rhyme | *An accusation
exercise: Which statement about an accusation is true? | *It can be true or false, and it still has to be proved | It is only a joke | It must always be true | It cannot be denied
test: What does it mean to deny an accusation? | To agree with it and say sorry | To repeat it to everyone | To write it down in a book | *To say that it is not true
test: Why should you not accuse someone because "everyone says"? | Because it is always rude to speak | *Because accusations are for a teacher to look into, with evidence | Because it makes a good joke | Because everyone is always wrong
exam: In the rhyme, «The Knave of Hearts, he stole those tarts», what is the accusation? | That it was a summer day | That the tarts were very sweet | *That the Knave stole the tarts | That the Queen made the tarts
